e to appreciate  is that for every child who we read  about who was through horrific suffering  there there has got to be untold number  of kids that are are harmed but don't  make headlines right I mean because part  of this is the is by its nature the type  of thing that kids are not going to  speak out about so we can only imagine  that for every one we hear about who  knows how many there are hundreds maybe  thousands that were not hearing about so  I kind of think that there are three  three areas here and you tell me if you  agree there is number one  you alluded to the sexual abuse  component which on this program we  particularly cover it visa vie the  Catholic Church but certainly it exists  across a number of religions then  there's the physical abuse which we've  talked about and there there were Act  there are actually some groups which  maybe you can speak to which have  manuals and guides which actually  dictate the physical punishment for  children and the number three is the  medical neglect which is choosing not to  obtain medical services for a child  either through the idea that prayer will  heal or through the idea that those  treatments go against God's will now of  the three which is the newest in other  words some elements of these I assume  have been around for a really long time  which of these are seeing the biggest  uprising lately well I want to first say  that you're missing one huge area which  i think is the most pervasive and that  is psychological and emotional neglect  entities often times we don't hear about  those cases even a religion is not  involved because they're next to  impossible to prove right it's hard to  occupy absolutely absolutely what you  you actually don't and in terms of  finding that someone a child has been  psychologically harmed it actually is  defined by the the harm itself the the  words that are used for example such as  you're a sinner and you're going to hell  or that kind of thing it's it actually  is not defined by proven results say you  know later on in life but I can almost  guarantee you at whenever you have a  case a religious child maltreatment that  involves sexual abuse physical abuse or  medical neglect you will you will have  psychological abuse for neglect as well  sure in fact the question of is one of  these something that we're seeing a  particularly drastic rise in lately no I  I wouldn't say that we are seeing  changes in the in the rate of these  things only because we're not able to  really track them I mean for example  when child protective services comes in  and interviews a family they're not  looking to see whether or not the  motivation behind the abuse or neglect  is a religious or spiritual  when someone is pulled over to the side  of the road by by a police officer and  they're given a ticket for speeding that  police officer will know whether or not  it's alcohol-related but we don't have  that same kind of tracking when it comes  to child abuse cases no other we did  have a story last week where a woman got  pulled over and actually said God was  telling her to go 100 miles per hour but  that seems got a little bit of an  isolated case now one thing people may  notice when they read the book and you  address this in the introduction is that  police officer doesn't have like they  they have a ready application with check  boxes and they have to know whether or  not it's a write it or not so I just  wanted to that's some I think we should  do in in terms of chocolate egg and  services but go ahead with your question  yeah well my question was going to be I  know that one of the criticisms will be  that hey you're focusing on Christianity  which of course Bill O'Reilly said a few  days ago is a philosophy and not a  religion but putting that aside for a  second that you focus on Christianity  you also talk about Judaism and Islam  but but there's going to be this claim  that hey are you focusing on  Christianity because it's the worst  o